# share of operand pairs whose result keeps a POINT denotation
   numeral  values    op     exact   inexact  out-of-range  undefined   exact share of in-range
    U<2,2>      16   add       136         0           120          0                  100.00%
    U<2,2>      16   sub       136         0           120          0                  100.00%
    U<2,2>      16   mul        90        72            94          0                   55.56%
    U<2,2>      16   div        74       142            24         16                   34.26%
    U<3,3>      64   add      2080         0          2016          0                  100.00%
    U<3,3>      64   sub      2080         0          2016          0                  100.00%
    U<3,3>      64   mul       554      1067          2475          0                   34.18%
    U<3,3>      64   div       490      3318           224         64                   12.87%
    U<4,4>     256   add     32896         0         32640          0                  100.00%
    U<4,4>     256   sub     32896         0         32640          0                  100.00%
    U<4,4>     256   mul      3170     12486         49880          0                   20.25%
    U<4,4>     256   div      2914     60446          1920        256                    4.60%

# reading
  Addition and subtraction keep the point denotation on every in-range pair,
  because the grid is closed under them. Multiplication and division do not, and
  the share that does is small and shrinks with the fraction width.

  So a strategy that demands a point denotation is not a marginal restriction on
  a numeral's operation set. It admits the additive part outright and refuses most
  of the multiplicative part, which is the same shape as the record's open
  question about Precise, arrived at from the denotation clause instead of from
  the strategy axis.
